  "body": "Part of #874. Found while working #874 (PR #1804). Related: #637, #589, #321.\n\n**What happens now.** PR #1804 carries the machined stock through a side-on quarter turn with a conservative read of the column body (`Body::carry_side`, `crates/sim/src/stock_sim/carry_side.rs`). The carried stock is never smaller than the real stock. The cost is this:\n\n- A wall that the tool flank cut, or a step, is read up to one grid cell outside the real face after the turn.\n- A cavity seen from the side (a pocket of the earlier setup) is filled.\n- The field engine refuses a side-on turn by name (`FIELD_SIDE_CARRY_REFUSAL`).\n\n**What a user can see.**\n\n- The verdict can show rest of up to one cell on a face that is finished.\n- The carried-fixture law (#321, `fixture_carried.rs`) can refuse a fixture that touches a turned face that was machined. The workaround is the setup's own stock.\n- Rest machining in the turned setup can cut air in a filled cavity.\n\n**The work.** Carry the stock side-on with the wall positions the engine knows, not the column pitch. One path is the field engine: re-index its 3-D bricks through the full signed permutation (`field_turn.rs` has the lattice turns only) and give it a column read that does not assume cuts along Z (`column_span`, `cut_ups`, `cut_downs`). The decision record `docs/decisions/874-side-on-carry.md` lists what was considered.\n\n**Rule that stays.** The carried stock is never smaller than the real stock. A turn that the engine cannot carry is refused by name.\n\n**Done means:** a plate with a contoured wall is carried side-on and the turned wall is read within the verdict tolerance, not one cell outside; a fixture in contact with that wall is not refused; a pocket of the earlier setup is still a cavity after the turn; a pinning test checks all three, and the field engine no longer refuses the side-on turn (or the issue says why it must).\n",
